------HCF------

1)Brute Force Approach:
package com.basics;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 10;
        int b = 15;

        // Initially, assume HCF is -1
        int gcf = -1;

        /*
         * Start checking from the smaller number
         * because HCF cannot be greater than
         * the smaller of the two numbers.
         */
        for (int i = Math.min(a, b); i >= 1; i--) {

            /*
             * If i divides both a and b,
             * then i is a common factor.
             */
            if (a % i == 0 && b % i == 0) {

                // Since we are checking from largest to smallest,
                // the first common factor is the HCF.
                gcf = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Print the HCF
        System.out.println(gcf);
    }
}
-->Brute Force Time Complexity : O(min(a,b))

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2)Using Subtraction:

package com.basics;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 10;
        int b = 15;

        /*
         * Keep subtracting the smaller number
         * from the larger number.
         *
         * Eventually, both numbers become equal.
         * That common value is the HCF.
         */
        while (a != b) {

            // If a is greater, subtract b from a
            if (a > b) {
                a = a - b;
            }

            // Otherwise, subtract a from b
            else {
                b = b - a;
            }
        }

        /*
         * When a == b,
         * that value is the HCF.
         */
        System.out.println(a);
    }
}
-->Subtraction  Time Complexity :   O(max(a,b))
